<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Model &middot; US Wildfires &middot; Robyn Collie</title>
<meta name="description" content="A bounded seasonal-risk model: state area burned vs drought, temperature, and precipitation. What it can say, what it can't, and where it fails.">
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg">
</head>
<body class="page-model">

<SiteHeader active="model" />

<main>

  <p class="callout-tag">Bounded tier &middot; technical note</p>
  <h1>A seasonal-risk model, and its limits</h1>
  <p class="dek">We fit one deliberately small model: does a season's drought, heat, and rainfall
  explain how many acres a state burns? It captures a modest, real signal &mdash; and this page is
  as much about where it breaks as where it works.</p>

  <!-- 1. WHAT THIS IS / IS NOT ------------------------------------------------ -->
  <section id="honesty" aria-labelledby="honesty-h">
    <h2 id="honesty-h"><span class="section-num">01</span>What this is &mdash; and what it is not</h2>
    <div class="callout honesty">
      <span class="callout-tag">Honesty block &mdash; verbatim from the model</span>
      <blockquote data-model-honesty>
        <!-- STAT: model_results.json _meta.honesty (verbatim) -->
        &ldquo;Prior art ceiling: Riley et al. 2013 PDSI-area R^2 ~ 0.25. This model explains state
        identity (FE) + a modest climate signal; it is a seasonal risk indicator, not a forecast of
        individual fires.&rdquo;
      </blockquote>
      <p class="src">&mdash; <code>analysis/model.py</code>, <code>_meta.honesty</code></p>
    </div>
    <p>To be explicit about the boundary, this model does <strong>not</strong> do any of the following:</p>
    <ul class="non-claims">
      <li><strong>No fire spread.</strong> It says nothing about how a given fire moves across a
      landscape, how fast, or in which direction. It is not a fire-behavior model.</li>
      <li><strong>No sub-seasonal forecasts.</strong> It relates a whole season's climate to that
      season's acres. It cannot tell you next week, or next month, is dangerous.</li>
      <li><strong>No individual-fire prediction.</strong> The unit of analysis is state &times; year.
      It never predicts whether, where, or when a single fire ignites.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>What it <em>is</em>: a coarse, held-out-validated indicator of how much a state's fire season
    tracks its drought and weather &mdash; useful for ranking relative risk, honest about its ceiling.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. SPEC + LOYO ----------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="spec" aria-labelledby="spec-h">
    <h2 id="spec-h"><span class="section-num">02</span>The specification</h2>
    <p>One pooled ordinary-least-squares regression. The outcome is log-transformed acres; the
    inputs are the season's drought, temperature, and precipitation, each standardized (z-scored);
    a fixed effect for each state absorbs its baseline fire level.</p>
    <p class="formula readout" role="img"
       aria-label="log base ten of acres plus one, regressed on z-scored PDSI, z-scored average temperature, z-scored precipitation over May through October, plus a state fixed effect.">
      <!-- STAT: model_results.json _meta.spec -->
      log<sub>10</sub>(acres + 1) &nbsp;=&nbsp; &beta;<sub>0</sub>
      + &beta;<sub>1</sub>&middot;z(PDSI) + &beta;<sub>2</sub>&middot;z(temp) + &beta;<sub>3</sub>&middot;z(precip)
      &nbsp;+&nbsp; state&nbsp;fixed&nbsp;effect &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="k">(May&ndash;Oct season)</span>
    </p>
    <p>The panel is small on purpose:
      <strong data-stat="panel.states"><!-- STAT: model_results.json panel.states -->38</strong> states,
      <strong data-stat="panel.years"><!-- STAT: model_results.json panel.years -->1992&ndash;2020</strong>,
      <strong data-stat="panel.rows"><!-- STAT: model_results.json panel.rows -->1,102</strong> state-year rows.</p>

    <h3>How we tested it honestly: leave-one-year-out</h3>
    <p>A model graded on the same data it learned from will always look better than it is. So we
    validated it the hard way &mdash; leave-one-year-out (LOYO) cross-validation. A non-statistician
    can follow it in four steps:</p>
    <ol class="loyo-steps">
      <li><strong>Hold out one year.</strong> Set aside every state's data for, say, 2017.</li>
      <li><strong>Train on the rest.</strong> Fit the model on all the <em>other</em> years only &mdash;
      it has never seen 2017.</li>
      <li><strong>Predict the held-out year.</strong> Use that model to predict 2017's acres, and
      compare to what actually burned.</li>
      <li><strong>Repeat for every year, then score.</strong> Rotate through all years so each is
      the held-out test once. The reported skill is measured only on these out-of-sample predictions.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Every number below the &ldquo;out-of-sample&rdquo; (OOS) label is a prediction the model made
    for data it was <em>not</em> trained on.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. LEAD NUMBER: SKILL VS CLIMATOLOGY ------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="skill" aria-labelledby="skill-h">
    <h2 id="skill-h"><span class="section-num">03</span>The number that matters: skill versus climatology</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      <span class="big-number" data-stat="skill_vs_climatology">
        <!-- STAT: model_results.json skill_vs_climatology -->+17.1%</span>
      reduction in prediction error versus a same-state climatology baseline, measured out-of-sample.</p>
    <p>The right question is not &ldquo;how much variance does the model explain?&rdquo; but
    &ldquo;does it beat the dumbest defensible guess?&rdquo; The dumb guess &mdash; the baseline &mdash;
    is <em>climatology</em>: predict each state's typical (median) burned acres from its training
    years, ignoring the weather entirely. Against that baseline, the climate model cuts prediction
    error by
    <strong data-stat="skill_vs_climatology"><!-- STAT: model_results.json skill_vs_climatology -->17.1%</strong>
    on held-out years. That is a modest, honest gain from knowing the season's drought and heat.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-tag">Why the raw R&sup2; flatters the model</span>
      <p>The overall out-of-sample R&sup2; is
      <strong data-stat="oos_r2_overall"><!-- STAT: model_results.json oos_r2_overall -->0.711</strong>,
      which sounds strong. Most of that is an illusion of the design: the <em>state fixed effects</em>
      do the heavy lifting. Knowing simply &ldquo;this is California&rdquo; versus &ldquo;this is
      Rhode Island&rdquo; already predicts wildly different acreage &mdash; before a single weather
      variable is added. So the 0.711 mostly measures the model's ability to tell states apart, not
      its ability to read the weather. The <strong>+17.1% skill over climatology</strong> is the
      honest measure of what the <em>climate signal</em> adds, because the climatology baseline
      already knows which state it is.</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Prior-art context.</strong> This ceiling is expected. Riley et&nbsp;al. (2013) related
      the Palmer Drought Severity Index to area burned across the western USA and found only a
      <strong>weak correlation, R&sup2; &asymp; 0.25</strong> <a href="#ref-riley2013">[riley2013]</a>
      &mdash; and showed that short-term fire-danger indices (like the Energy Release Component)
      outperform PDSI. A drought-based seasonal model is <em>supposed</em> to explain a modest share
      of the variance. Ours claiming more would be a red flag, not a triumph.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. WHERE IT WORKS / FAILS ------------------------------------------------ -->
  <section id="where" aria-labelledby="where-h">
    <h2 id="where-h"><span class="section-num">04</span>Where it works, and where it fails</h2>
    <p>Skill is not uniform. Broken out by state (each state's own out-of-sample R&sup2;), the model
    reads drought-driven Western fire regimes well and fails in fuel-limited or human-dominated ones.</p>

    <figure class="chart full-bleed">
      <div id="state-r2-tilemap" class="wf-tilemap" role="img"
           aria-label="US tile-grid map of per-state out-of-sample R-squared. California, Colorado, South Dakota and the Mountain West score highest; Nevada, Missouri, South Carolina and Mississippi are negative.">
        <!-- WF.tilemap renders here from model_results per_state_oos_r2. Static tables below are the no-JS fallback. -->
      </div>
      <p class="data-fallback" hidden>Interactive map unavailable &mdash; the per-state values are in the tables below.</p>
      <figcaption class="src">Per-state out-of-sample R&sup2;. Cool tiles = negative (worse than
      climatology); ember tiles = positive skill. Source: <code>model_results.json</code>,
      <code>per_state_oos_r2</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Where it works &mdash; top 10 states by OOS R&sup2;</h3>
    <div class="data-table-wrap">
      <table class="data" id="top10-table">
        <caption>Top ten states by out-of-sample R-squared (Source: model_results.json, per_state_oos_r2_top10)</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">OOS R&sup2;</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <!-- STAT: model_results.json per_state_oos_r2_top10 (verbatim) -->
          <tr><td>California</td><td class="num">0.480</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Colorado</td><td class="num">0.386</td></tr>
          <tr><td>South Dakota</td><td class="num">0.379</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Montana</td><td class="num">0.313</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wyoming</td><td class="num">0.282</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Texas</td><td class="num">0.247</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Georgia</td><td class="num">0.216</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Oregon</td><td class="num">0.213</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tennessee</td><td class="num">0.204</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Washington</td><td class="num">0.203</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Where it fails &mdash; the negative states</h3>
    <div class="data-table-wrap">
      <table class="data" id="bottom5-table">
        <caption>Five worst states by out-of-sample R-squared (Source: model_results.json, per_state_oos_r2_bottom5)</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">OOS R&sup2;</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <!-- STAT: model_results.json per_state_oos_r2_bottom5 (verbatim) -->
          <tr><td>Oklahoma</td><td class="num">&minus;0.098</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nevada</td><td class="num">&minus;0.163</td></tr>
          <tr><td>South Carolina</td><td class="num">&minus;0.302</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Missouri</td><td class="num">&minus;0.324</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mississippi</td><td class="num">&minus;0.625</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>A negative R&sup2; means the model does <em>worse</em> than simply guessing the state's usual
    acreage. In these states the drought signal is either absent or points the wrong way.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-tag">Nevada, and the fuel-limitation wrinkle</span>
      <p>Nevada's
      <strong data-stat="per_state_oos_r2_bottom5.NV"><!-- STAT: model_results.json per_state_oos_r2_bottom5 (NV = -0.163) -->&minus;0.163</strong>
      is not noise &mdash; it is the model meeting its own assumption head-on. The model encodes the
      Western logic that <em>drier means more fire</em>. But the Great Basin is
      <strong>fuel-limited</strong>, not flammability-limited: its sparse desert grasses only carry
      fire after a <em>wet</em> year grows enough of them, so the biggest burns often follow the
      rain, not the drought. That inversion is exactly the wrinkle laid out on the
      <a href="/drivers.html#wrinkle">Drivers page</a> &mdash; and the model, built on the
      drought-drives-fire premise, gets Nevada backwards for precisely that reason. It is an honest
      failure that points at real physics.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. COEFFICIENTS ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="coefficients" aria-labelledby="coef-h">
    <h2 id="coef-h"><span class="section-num">05</span>Coefficients: do the signs make physical sense?</h2>
    <p>A model can score well for the wrong reasons. The cleanest sanity check is whether the signs
    of the coefficients match physical intuition. They do. Values are on z-scored inputs, so the
    magnitudes are directly comparable &mdash; each is the change in log<sub>10</sub>(acres) per one
    standard deviation of that variable.</p>
    <div class="data-table-wrap">
      <table class="data" id="coef-table">
        <caption>Standardized regression coefficients and their physical interpretation (Source: model_results.json, coefficients_z)</caption>
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">Variable</th><th scope="col">z-coefficient</th>
          <th scope="col">Sign</th><th scope="col">Physically&hellip;</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <!-- STAT: model_results.json coefficients_z (verbatim) -->
          <tr>
            <td>PDSI (drought index)</td>
            <td class="num">&minus;0.1013</td>
            <td>&minus;</td>
            <td>Correct. Higher PDSI = <em>wetter</em>, so more moisture &rarr; fewer acres burned.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Average temperature</td>
            <td class="num">+0.0915</td>
            <td>+</td>
            <td>Correct. Hotter seasons &rarr; more acres burned.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Precipitation</td>
            <td class="num">&minus;0.0617</td>
            <td>&minus;</td>
            <td>Correct. More rain &rarr; fewer acres burned.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Drought (PDSI) carries the largest standardized weight, temperature next, precipitation
    smallest &mdash; a physically coherent ordering for area burned at the seasonal scale.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. WORST MISSES ---------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="misses" aria-labelledby="misses-h">
    <h2 id="misses-h"><span class="section-num">06</span>The honesty exhibit: worst misses</h2>
    <p>These are the five predictions the model got most wrong. In every case it predicted a large
    burn where almost nothing happened &mdash; small Plains and Eastern states in the early 1990s.</p>
    <div class="data-table-wrap">
      <table class="data" id="misses-table">
        <caption>Five worst prediction errors: predicted versus actual acres (Source: model_results.json, worst_misses)</caption>
        <thead><tr>
          <th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Year</th>
          <th scope="col">Actual acres</th><th scope="col">Predicted acres</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <!-- STAT: model_results.json worst_misses (verbatim) -->
          <tr><td>Kansas</td><td class="num">1992</td><td class="num">251</td><td class="num">16,116</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nebraska</td><td class="num">1993</td><td class="num">173</td><td class="num">6,262</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pennsylvania</td><td class="num">1995</td><td class="num">51</td><td class="num">1,721</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pennsylvania</td><td class="num">1993</td><td class="num">40</td><td class="num">1,320</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Kentucky</td><td class="num">1993</td><td class="num">843</td><td class="num">26,393</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-tag">Read these in log space</span>
      <p>The model is fit on log<sub>10</sub>(acres + 1), so it is penalized for getting the
      <em>order of magnitude</em> wrong, not the raw acre gap. A predicted 16,116 against an actual
      251 is a miss of roughly two orders of magnitude &mdash; large on the scale the model actually
      optimizes &mdash; even though, against a ten-million-acre Western fire year, 16,000 acres is a
      rounding error. These are the biggest <em>relative</em> misses, which is why small, low-fire
      states dominate the list.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Scatter (progressive enhancement) ---------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="scatter" aria-labelledby="scatter-h">
    <h2 id="scatter-h"><span class="section-num">07</span>Predicted versus actual (the worst misses, plotted)</h2>
    <figure class="chart">
      <div id="misses-scatter" class="wf-scatter" role="img"
           aria-label="Scatter of all 1,102 held-out predictions: predicted versus actual acres on log scales, with a one-to-one reference line. The cloud tracks the line with wide spread; the five worst misses sit far above it.">
        <!-- WF.scatter renders ALL cv_predictions vs a 1:1 line. Falls back to the table above. -->
      </div>
      <p class="data-fallback">This chart requires JavaScript. A five-row excerpt (the worst misses)
      is in the <a href="#misses-table">table</a> above; the full 1,102-row validation set is in
      <code>/data/model_results.json</code>.</p>
      <figcaption class="src">Every point is one held-out state-year prediction (1,102 in all
      &mdash; each made by a model that never saw that year); the diagonal is a perfect prediction.
      The cloud hugs the line loosely &mdash; that spread <em>is</em> the modest skill &mdash; and the
      five worst misses from the table sit far above it. Source:
      <code>model_results.json</code>, <code>cv_predictions</code>. <!-- STAT: model_results.json cv_predictions.rows (n=1102) --></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. WHAT WOULD MAKE IT BETTER --------------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="better" aria-labelledby="better-h">
    <h2 id="better-h"><span class="section-num">08</span>What would make it better</h2>
    <p>The point of a bounded model is knowing its next move. Three would matter most:</p>
    <ul class="improvements">
      <li><strong>Gridded fuel data.</strong> Fuel load and continuity explain why Nevada burns after
      wet years; a drought index alone cannot see the grass.</li>
      <li><strong>Sub-seasonal fire-danger indices (e.g.&nbsp;ERC).</strong> Short-term indices track
      area burned better than PDSI does <a href="#ref-riley2013">[riley2013]</a>, and would move the
      model toward operational timescales.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignition modeling.</strong> Most fires are human-started; a model with no ignition
      term is blind to the spark, only the conditions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each is a direction, not a promise &mdash; and none would change what this page opens with:
    this is a seasonal risk indicator, not a forecast of individual fires.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- REFERENCES --------------------------------------------------------------- -->
  <section id="references" class="references" aria-labelledby="ref-h">
    <h2 id="ref-h">References</h2>
    <ol>
      <li id="ref-riley2013"><span class="ref-key">[riley2013]</span> Riley, K.L., Abatzoglou, J.T.,
      Grenfell, I.C., Klene, A.E., &amp; Heinsch, F.A. (2013). The relationship of large fire
      occurrence with drought and fire danger indices in the western USA, 1984&ndash;2008: The role
